WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.700
Our leaders are stupid.

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.560
Our politicians are stupid.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:09.940
Donald Trump, who four short years ago was viewed by many GOP operatives as an erratic outsider,

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.420
has just been renominated as the party's standard bearer.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.580
But whether he wins or loses, can a party without any guiding principles survive?

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.720
How do the political parties die? And how are new ones born?

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:29.940
Imagine a political party that has lost its ideological coherence and is torn apart by various warring factions,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.560
then an outsider and celebrity candidate emerges

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.560
with no fealty to the party's policy agenda

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.100
and with no previous political experience.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.100
He goes on to connect with voters

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.100
and retake the White House.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.100
That's exactly what happened...

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.640
...in 1848

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.640
when the Whigs backed Zachary Taylor.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.160
Political scientist Philip Wallach

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.660
is a resident senior fellow at the think tank the R Street Institute.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.260
He published a 2017 paper on the parallels between Taylor and Trump

